Parent-element mismatch as a type conflict

When two ConcreteTypes get assigned to the same equivalence class with
the same TypeId but parent elements that fall in distinct classes,
Structure::close used to enqueue pairwise parent equalities, silently
collapsing the ambient parents. That is wrong in the multi-model
setting: distinct ambient parents must not be unified just because a
type was imposed twice.

drain_equalities and impose_concrete_type now leave parents alone and
record a TypeConflict instead. Detection is deferred: the merge step
queues a DeferredParentCheck (local to Structure::close) whenever the
TypeIds match but parents currently disagree, and after the
drain/functionality/typing fixed point converges the queue is
re-evaluated under the final unification. Only entries whose parents
still disagree become a TypeConflict, so a still-pending equate that
brings the parents together is no longer mistaken for a conflict.

TypeConflict now carries both full ConcreteTypes (TypeId plus parent
ElId list) so callers can inspect the offending parents. A new
CompileError::ConflictingParentEl variant reports the locations of
the two terms backing the innermost (highest-index) differing pair of
parent elements; the existing ConflictingTermType is kept for true
TypeId mismatches.
